  <article class="article">
    <h1>Wholesale: What It Means</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Wholesale means buying incense in bulk rather than at retail. This page explains the concept
      and the questions that matter, in neutral terms — there is no catalog, no quote process, and
      nothing to order here.
    </p>

    <h2>The formats</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Incense sticks</strong> (线香) — the common modern format</li>
      <li><strong>Incense cones</strong> (塔香) and <strong>coils</strong> (盘香)</li>
      <li><strong>Sachets</strong> (香囊)</li>
      <li><strong>Raw materials</strong> (香材) — woods, resins, herbs</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>The questions that matter</h2>
    <p>
      For any bulk purchase, the checks are the same ones that run through the whole knowledge base:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Sample against the specification.</strong> Evaluate aroma, format, material, and
        burn against the written spec before committing to volume. See the
        <a href="/blog/agarwood-powder-vs-chips/">form-and-use</a> reference.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verify the material.</strong> For high-value botanicals (sandalwood, agarwood),
        confirm species and grade rather than trusting the trade name. See the
        <a href="/blog/incense-authentication-database/">authentication database</a> and the
        <a href="/blog/incense-substitution-cross-reference/">substitution cross-reference</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storage and moisture.</strong> Botanical incense loses aroma when exposed to air and
        moisture; packaging must seal the product for shelf life. See
        <a href="/blog/incense-storage-longevity/">storage &amp; longevity</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Destination requirements.</strong> Import rules for plant material, combustion
        products, and labeling vary by market. See
        <a href="/blog/incense-export-considerations/">export considerations</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      For the material identity behind any bulk order, begin at the
      <a href="/ingredients/">ingredient reference</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
